Integration exports
- declaration.ts: hempiProviderDeclaration(): PluginProviderDeclaration. Base id hempi, displayName Hempi, family agents, supportsManualCompaction false. server/providers.ts registers plain Hempi and one `agents` provider with a bounded profile catalog (model IDs `agent/<UUID>`), replacing per-agent provider registrations on catalog change.
- host.ts: experimental_providerBridge, experimental_nativeRootsHostContract, resolvePiNativeRoots. Its default entry implements native roots and the public typed agent-memory host contract.
- server/providers.ts derives bound agent identity, frozen memory key, persona, level/pin and thread ID for Agents turns. Its environment hook issues a per-turn memory capability; plain Hempi derives thread ID and optional longMemory.

Lifecycle and policy
- Requires Pi 1.2.0 for agent_settled and handled-prompt disposition semantics.
- Thread construction and idle model/persona changes store options without acquiring an OptChat profile lock. Agents expose agent/<UUID> IDs in BB; level selection resolves against the execution host's unbound Codex catalog. An explicitly available compatible Gateway route can be pinned with supported effort. Every managed turn requires the host's Gateway extension and BB-supplied URL/token, including openai-codex selections; route identifiers do not enable native-auth fallback. Unsupported or unavailable pins fail closed.
- Each main turn starts Pi against the same native --session file. agent_end closes output/checkpoints but does not settle the BB turn. agent_settled settles the turn, then stdin/fd4 close and the bridge waits for child exit before publishing its turn boundary. A handled prompt response bypasses model-event settlement and follows the same shutdown path.
- Idle state is cached in the bridge. Model/persona/environment changes replace the stored session configuration, keeping its native session path. Resume/edit reconstruction and fork/relocation use the existing native session and unbound short-lived fork helper as needed.
- Model catalogs and helpers omit OptChat flags. Launch override arguments containing --optchat-* are rejected; profile binding is owned by providerOptions. Agents catalog lists profile IDs, not Pi models. The bridge validates a bound agent ID and its Pi model before main prompts, resume and steering. This does not sandbox arbitrary installed Pi extensions or their independent SDK children.
- Agent turns bind the persisted memory key (seeded as agent-<old handle> for existing profiles) with --optchat-create and --optchat-instructions. Rename preserves this key. Instructions are written under the host's persistent plugin dataDir and refreshed when text changes. Native automatic compaction is disabled for Agents and unexpected compaction fails visibly.
- Plain long-memory turns bind thread-<threadId> with --optchat-create. Existing native history gets --optchat-import-session on its first successful binding only. If no native session file exists, import is omitted: there is no history to import and no fabricated session is written. A successful empty binding is still recorded, so later turns do not import their own new history.
- Successful binding markers are keyed by native session file and profile under dataDir. Startup failure never writes a marker. Off/on toggles do not reset it. With long memory off, no OptChat flags are sent and Pi's native automatic compaction is available again.
- Exit 75 backs off from one second to a maximum of fifteen seconds for at most ten minutes. Visible Waiting for <agent>… entries explain retries. Expiry is a clear failed request. Cancellation aborts sleeps and active startup. Exit 65/66 fails immediately with the drained stderr line. Native recovery refusal exits 73, is not retried as contention, and names the retained copy and BB recovery action.
- notify and changed nonempty setStatus values become public provider.warning/general deltas. SDK 0.6.37 exposes this visible thread-info route, not a separate info delta. Repeated status text for the same statusKey is suppressed; clearing removes the deduplication entry.
- Fork helpers do not bind memory. Agent forks still use the same agent profile on their next turn. Plain long-memory forks derive a new thread profile and import the fork's native history once.
- The launcher performs an offline --help capability probe without OptChat flags. If both --role and --optchat-profile are registered, an installed Hempi is already active and the embedded copy is not loaded. Otherwise the bridge passes --extension <host-dataDir/bundled-source/src/index.ts>. Installed auto-loaded Hempi must include this pinned revision's settlement, Gateway lookup and recovery-marker enforcement, plus the local original-prompt patch. Capability flags alone do not certify its version or provenance.
- Local turns preflight pending recovery, then Pi owns the primary native profile lock directly; no local profile clone is made. Remote turns hold the exact pinned Hempi OS lock across snapshot, execution, Pi exit and acknowledged copy-back. Busy preflight uses the existing visible ten-minute retry policy.
- server/agent-memory.ts issues thread/host/profile-scoped HMAC capabilities through a provider environment hook. Pi and helpers never receive these control secrets. Signed HTTP requests carry at most 128 KiB; regular-file chunks are at most 64 KiB with four in flight. Typed public host RPC reads/writes the selected profile, including dotfiles, binary data, empty files/directories and Git checkpoints. Hashes verify the complete manifest and file contents. Traversal, symlinks and special files are rejected; OS sockets are excluded.
- Remote OPTCHAT_HOME is private under host dataDir/memory-transfers/<operation>/home. The primary agent profile remains in primary OPTCHAT_HOME. No other profiles, last-profile, Pi auth or project credentials are transferred. Plain Hempi thread memory is still host-local.
- Pi exit precedes the durable remote ready marker. Installation keeps the canonical profile directory/socket stable and uses staged files, rollback journals, fsync and a durable commit receipt. Successful transfers remove bulky operation manifests; small receipts remain for lost-reply acknowledgement. Completed remote working copies are deleted.
- Before a remote acquire returns, the primary worker atomically persists the authoritative journal and profile-local remote-pending.json under the native lock. The guard and its interrupted atomic-write files are excluded from snapshots, transfers, manifests and base digests. The guard remains in the canonical directory through installation, so worker death cannot expose stale or partially installed memory to plain Pi. Native binding refuses it with exit 73 and recovery guidance.
- Copy-back failure fails the BB turn and retains the remote ready copy. The next agent turn recovers under the primary lock before allowing local or remote execution. A matching already-installed incoming digest or durable receipt completes interrupted commit without a false base conflict, even if the remote is offline. A journal that never reached running is a proven pre-start cancellation and clears its marker under the lock. Otherwise offline hosts, unconfirmed exit, hash mismatch, changed central files or corrupt receipts fail closed. Never erase journals or retained copies to bypass recovery.
- Heartbeats cancel a remote Pi when authority is lost. The server does not time-steal a running lease: stale prepared operations or a remote ready marker can release it safely; unresolved execution stays blocked. Byte logs contain raw downloaded/uploaded counts per operation, not file contents or capabilities.

Host locality
- The provider worker launches pi from its own PATH. HOME and PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR belong to that worker host. BB_PI_BRIDGE_COMMAND/ARGS are optional launch overrides, not required for normal operation.
- Install Pi >=1.2.0 on every execution host. The pinned Hempi extension materializes from host.js under host dataDir. A separate installed shim, if present, must point to that host's compatible Hempi source. Use the Pi binary directly so BB tool-channel fd3/fd4 survive.
- Managed turns require the Model Gateway Pi extension and BB-supplied runtime URL/token on that host. Native openai-codex authentication is not a fallback. Other harness logins do not supply the Gateway route. Primary OPTCHAT_HOME is resolved by the retained Hempi implementation; it defaults to ~/.optchat.
- Persona files, successful import markers, bundled extension files, transfer journals and retained recovery copies use context.dataDir on their owning host. Generated BB tool/extension files use context.tempDir. Native sessions default to that host's ~/.bb/pi-bridge-sessions, with BB_PI_BRIDGE_SESSION_DIR as an override. These directories must be writable and persistent.
- Project CLI credentials, skills, browser tools and mounted workspaces remain execution-host prerequisites. BB plugin API credentials stay with their owning server tools and do not need to be copied into Pi.

Offline verification
- bridge/hempi.lifecycle.test.ts covers per-turn process/session reuse, delayed final settlement, handled notifications/status, persona refresh, long-memory import-once/off/absent-session/failure, busy retry/cancel/error paths, clean catalog/health/fork helpers and model rejection.
- optchat.test.ts covers ten-minute expiry/backoff and cancellation before the next attempt.
- bridge/fake-pi-rpc.mjs is a self-contained offline RPC fixture. It loads neither Pi nor a model SDK and makes no network calls. It is not a substitute for a real Pi/Hempi integration check.
- memory.test.ts exercises the real pinned OS lock and signed HTTP/typed host transport across isolated host directories: binary/hidden profile data, byte logs, concurrent exclusion, failed copy-back, offline recovery, manual-write protection, host restart, both interrupted install phases, lost commit replies, HMAC/replay/traversal rejection and cancellation during acquisition.
- bridge/hempi.memory-lifecycle.test.ts exercises BB turn boundaries against fake Pi: exit-time writes survive into another thread, boundaries wait for verified commit, failed copy-back fails the turn, and cancellation acknowledges only after Pi exit and copy-back.
- Every spawning fixture isolates HOME, USERPROFILE, OPTCHAT_HOME and PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR, uses an empty PATH, asserts the exact fake launcher, and rejects a missing launcher override before discovery. Real Pi/model calls are forbidden in these tests.

Standalone fake-Pi smoke inputs
- Set BB_PI_BRIDGE_COMMAND to process.execPath and BB_PI_BRIDGE_ARGS to JSON.stringify([absolutePathToThisDirectory + '/bridge/fake-pi-rpc.mjs']).
- Set HOME, USERPROFILE, OPTCHAT_HOME and PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR below an owned temporary directory; set PATH to an empty directory and FAKE_PI_TEST_ROOT to that temporary root. Assert resolvePiLaunch returns exactly the absolute Node executable and this fake script before bridge startup; never fall back to real Pi. Set BB_PI_BRIDGE_SESSION_DIR to a temporary sessions directory and supply temporary bridge dataDir/tempDir.
- FAKE_PI_PROCESS_LOG: path receiving spawn:<pid> and exit:<pid> lines.
- FAKE_PI_LAUNCH_LOG: path receiving JSONL {pid,args}, including startup failures and unbound helpers.
- FAKE_PI_ATTEMPT_FILE: persistent integer counter; combine with FAKE_PI_BUSY_ATTEMPTS=N to exit 75 on the first N profile-bound starts.
- FAKE_PI_STARTUP_EXIT=65, 66 or 73: fail startup with the corresponding OptChat stderr line. A real fixture recovery marker also exits 73. Unset or empty disables the override.
- FAKE_PI_END_MARKER and FAKE_PI_SETTLED_MARKER: paths written around the delayed-settle gap.
- A normal prompt streams a fake response, writes native history, emits agent_end, then agent_settled. /handled emits notify + duplicate setStatus, responds disposition handled, and emits no agent events. /delayed-settle waits 200ms between agent_end and agent_settled. /hold waits until abort.
- Plain Hempi thread start params: {threadId:'thr_smoke', cwd:temporaryWorkspace, instructionMode:'append', options:{permissionMode:'full',permissionScope:'full',approvalReviewer:null,permissionEscalation:null,model:'openai-codex/fake-model',providerOptions:{threadId:'thr_smoke',longMemory:true,instructions:'Offline persona'}}}. Agents smoke instead requires a bound `agent/<UUID>` identity, matching options, a real signed coordinator fixture and an agent catalog; an unbound launch fails closed.
- Turn params: {threadId:'thr_smoke',providerThreadId:returnedProviderThreadId,clientRequestId:'creq_abcdefghjk',input:[{type:'text',text:'/delayed-settle',mentions:[]}],options:sameOptionsAsThreadStart}. After its turn.boundary, send another turn with a new valid clientRequestId and text '/handled'.
- Expected: two distinct main child pids, identical --session path, separate --provider openai-codex --model fake-model arguments, exit for each pid before its final turn.boundary, visible OptChat/handled/status entries, and no need for real models or credentials.
